ed value. The opponent has shown no strength. Betting is a bluff that may be called by worse cards. However, if the pot is very small, then my hand (Pair of Aces, King, Ten, Eight) has showdown value, so I should check back.\n\n**Answer:** 0\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board is Ace of Hearts, 8 of Diamonds, King of Diamonds, Ace of Spades. You hold a 2 of Spades and 10 of Hearts. Your best hand is a Pair of Aces with a King, Ten, and Eight kicker (A-A-K-10-8). This is a weak hand, relying on the board significantly. However, the opponent has shown passivity by checking pre-flop, on the flop, and now on the turn. There is very little money in the pot. Betting (bluffing) has little upside because the pot is small, and you might get called by a King, an 8, or a better high card. Checking allows you to realize your equity for free and see the River. You should never fold when checking is an option.\n\nReason: With a weak hand and a small pot, checking is the most prudent play to minimize loss and potentially win a showdown against a lower kicker without risking additional chips.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
